🔹 1. git init

Usage:

git init


What it does:
Initializes a new Git repository in your current directory. It creates a .git folder.

When to use:
When starting a new project and want to track it using Git.

🔹 2. git clone

Usage:

git clone <repository_url>


What it does:
Downloads a copy of a remote repository to your local system.

Example:

git clone https://github.com/user/project.git

🔹 3. git status

Usage:

git status


What it does:
Shows the current state of the working directory:

Modified files

New files

Staged vs unstaged changes

🔹 4. git add

Usage:

git add <file>
git add .


What it does:
Moves changes from working directory → staging area.

Examples:

git add file.txt     # Adds one file
git add .            # Adds all files

🔹 5. git commit

Usage:

git commit -m "Your message"


What it does:
Saves staged changes to the local repository with a message.

Example:

git commit -m "Fixed login bug"

🔹 6. git push

Usage:

git push origin <branch>


What it does:
Uploads your local commits to the remote repository.

Example:

git push origin main

🔹 7. git pull

Usage:

git pull


What it does:
Fetches and merges changes from the remote repo into your local branch.

Equivalent to:

git fetch + git merge

🔹 8. git fetch

Usage:

git fetch


What it does:
Downloads changes from remote but DOES NOT merge automatically.

🔹 9. git branch
➤ List branches
git branch

➤ Create branch
git branch <branch-name>

➤ Delete branch
git branch -d <branch-name>

🔹 10. git checkout
➤ Switch branches
git checkout <branch-name>

➤ Create + switch
git checkout -b <branch-name>

🔹 11. git merge

Usage:

git merge <branch>


What it does:
Merges another branch into your current branch.

Example:

git merge dev

🔹 12. git log

Usage:

git log


What it does:
Shows commit history.

Shorter format:
git log --oneline

🔹 13. git reset
➤ Reset staged file (soft reset)
git reset <file>

➤ Undo commit but keep changes
git reset --soft HEAD~1

➤ Undo commit & remove changes
git reset --hard HEAD~1

🔹 14. git revert

Usage:

git revert <commit-id>


What it does:
Creates a NEW commit that reverses an old commit.

Safer than reset.

🔹 15. git stash
➤ Save changes temporarily
git stash

➤ View stashes
git stash list

➤ Apply stash
git stash apply

🔹 16. git remote
➤ List remotes
git remote -v

➤ Add remote
git remote add origin <url>

🔹 17. git diff

Usage:

git diff              # unstaged changes
git diff --staged     # staged changes


Shows line-by-line differences.

🔹 18. git rm

Usage:

git rm <file>


Deletes a file and stages the deletion.

🔹 19. git tag
➤ Create tag
git tag v1.0

➤ Push tag
git push origin v1.0

🔹 20. git config
➤ Set username and email
git config --global user.name "Nikhil"
git config --global user.email "nikhil@example.com"

➤ Check config
git config --list